<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  mixed_district — "diverse topology" network (IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md §4;
  STEPS.md Step 18), a deliberate contrast to grid_4x4's regular grid.

  Generated with netgenerate's "rand" topology mode (random topology,
  seed=42) plus a hand-edit via netconvert's plain-XML workflow (node/edge
  files, never hand-typed net.xml) to splice in a 5-arm roundabout; see the
  generation notes kept alongside this scenario for the exact commands
  and verification. The network structurally guarantees, per Step 18's
  spec:
    - at least one internal (non-fringe) T-junction (degree-3 node), e.g. "8"
    - at least one roundabout (5-node ring, junctions rr_2/rr_205/rr_266/
      rr_276/rr_358), confirmed via sumolib's net.getRoundabouts()
    - at least one offset junction ("nut lech": no pair of edges ~180
      degrees apart, so no straight through-movement), e.g. junction "1"

  29 nodes total, 9 signalized junctions (12 signalized in grid_4x4, same
  order of magnitude). Traffic demand: 2000 veh/h over 3600s, generated by
  randomTrips.py with seed=42 (period=1.8s). This is a LONGER period
  (lighter demand) than grid_4x4's 0.8s because this irregular/random
  topology has less redundant capacity per vehicle than a regular grid (a
  single-lane roundabout ring, several dead-end fringe edges, no uniform
  alternate-route structure). Tuned empirically via sim/runner.py's actual
  stop condition (drains until simulation.getMinExpectedNumber()==0, not a
  hard cutoff at end=3600): grid_4x4's own 0.8s period, and even this
  network's own 1.5s/2400-veh period, left enough residual congestion that
  the last vehicles took until sim_time ~7000-9500s to clear (long
  teleport-heavy tail, no fatal errors, just an unrealistically long
  drain). 2000 veh/h (period=1.8s) was the highest demand found that still
  drains close to grid_4x4's own tail (~3750-3810s here vs. ~3820s there,
  across fixed/actuated/maxpressure). A handful of teleport warnings still
  occur (same as grid_4x4: expected under congestion, not a fatal error),
  just nowhere near the earlier long-tail behavior.

  The seed option is intentionally NOT hardcoded here - it is passed at run
  time (via SumoConnection.start(seed=...) or the sumo CLI seed flag) so the
  comparison harness (STEPS.md Step 9) can run the same scenario across
  multiple seeds.

  device.rerouting.probability=1.0 equips every vehicle with a rerouting
  device up front, so VMS / route-advisory actions (STEPS.md Step 19) can
  actually change a vehicle's path later without re-touching this config.
-->
<sumoConfiguration xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="http://sumo.dlr.de/xsd/sumoConfiguration.xsd">

    <input>
        <net-file value="net.xml"/>
        <route-files value="trips.xml"/>
    </input>

    <time>
        <begin value="0"/>
        <end value="3600"/>
        <step-length value="1"/>
    </time>

    <processing>
        <device.rerouting.probability value="1.0"/>
    </processing>

</sumoConfiguration>
